Introduction | What is ADM and why it matters

Automated Decision Making (ADM) is the use of computer systems to make or 
assist in making decisions. There are systems that employ fixed rules and 
there are systems using machine learning. Government ADM is capable of 
handling a high volume of cases in a regular, rapid manner. However, 
choices regarding benefits, immigration and services and eligibility have
an impact on people's rights and wellbeing. As an emerging ICT professional,
I must consider accuracy, bias, privacy, explanation, and human review and
challenge a decision. The Australian experience of Robodebt underscores the 
importance of the legal authority and accountability. To contrast Australia's
reforms at the moment with other alternatives, and to state how my understanding has evolved.

Artefact 1  Australia: automated decision-making reform consultation (2024–2025)
 Summary. This consultation by the Australian Government sought input from the
public between November 2024 and January 2025 on safeguards for automated
decision making by government. It was tied to the recommendations made by th
e Robodebt Royal Commission. The feedback summary published states that a group 
of community organisations, legal groups, government bodies, and others were
involved in the reform discussion. The challenge is how can government do this
without compromising transparency, fairness or review opportunities?

 Justification and reflection. I chose this artefact because I believe that
the issue of ADM is not just a programming problem; it's also a law-related 
issue, and a public participation issue. It relates to Kantianism as people 
shouldn't be treated as block data without being explained or having a review 
path. I discovered that involving communities in the pre-development process
of automated systems can uncover risks which developers may overlook. This is 
of course meaningful evidence of my evolving education in the field of responsible
governments technology.

Artefact 2 – Australia: privacy regulator’s submission (2025)
 Summary. The OAIC provided its feedback on the consultation
in January 2025 regarding the national ADM. The feedback includes broad
coverage of the automated systems, pro-active information sharing, 
meaningful explanations and protections throughout the life cycle 
of the system. Privacy, access to information, contracted service 
providers and accountability are all covered. This is vital for 
  two reasons. Firstly, individuals may not be aware that the 
computer-generated recommendations have had an effect on the government
decision.

Support and evaluation. I selected this source as it mentions the 
relationship between privacy and procedural fairness. It helped me
realize that the ‘a computer was used' statement is not sufficient. 
They need to be provided with reasons that are easy to follow, 
understandable, and acceptable to them. This is related to the ACS
Code statement of public interest, honesty and professional competence.
Now, as an ICT student, I would ask if an important decision is made,
is it recorded in a system, and if people can get clear explanations, 
can they get explanations that do not expose other people's private data?

Artefact 3 – Australia: AI assurance framework (2024)

 Summary. The framework for the national assurance of artificial
intelligence in government in Australia has been launched in June 2024.
It provides guidance on common assurance approaches and techniques 
aimed at assisting governments in implementing the AI ethical guidelines. 
It is applicable to ADM where artificial intelligence is applied to 
categorize, forecast or suggest a certain result.

 Justification and reflection. The inclusion of this artefact is justified 
by its capacity to operationalise ethical concepts in the context of project 
teams. Such questions are for instance what will happen in terms of quality 
control of data, how a high risk system will be approved, and how will
performance be managed after implementation? Act Utilitarianism is applicable 
in this context as risks and gains should be assessed before the effects on
people. I have found out that ethical work with ICT does not end with the 
launch of a product.

Artefact 4 – Australia: human rights safeguards for ADM (2025)

 Summary. The Australian Human Rights Commission Submission 2025 suggests 
that ADM by the government must respect human rights. Its recommendations 
include impact assessments of human rights, informing individuals when there
is any substantial impact on their lives through ADM, and providing rational
explanations for its decisions. The submission highlights the risks of automation 
reproducing disadvantage due to poor design or management of the system.

Reason for selection and reflection. I chose this artifact since it provides 
a human perspective on technology. It was evident from my reflection that mere 
accuracy will not determine the dignity of humanity in a decision-making process. 
The Kantian theory offers insight into why a person deserves an opportunity to 
know about and question an adverse decision made against him or her. This artifact
has influenced my perception of professional achievement since a successful system
is one that is quick and accessible to the users.

Artefact 5 – United Kingdom: algorithmic transparency standard (2025)

 Summary. The UK published revised guidance on the Algorithmic Transparency
Recording Standard in May 2025. This standard allows public bodies to publish
details about the algorithmic technologies they employ, why they employ them,
and their role in decision-making. These details include both a summary meant 
for the public and technical information. There is also an identification of 
those organizations and tools for which there are obligations to publish.

Justification and Reflection. I have selected this example from 
the international perspective because it offers a tangible transparency mechanism
that Australia could adopt. This is an example which demonstrates that transparency
could be managed via routine public documents instead of being based on abstract
assurances. I came to realize that clear explanations of the purpose, responsibility
and constraints could enable the citizens to analyze the system. This point is
related to the ACS principle of honesty. Nevertheless, publication of the document 
is just one element.

Artefact 6 – Canada: Directive on Automated Decision-Making (2025 update)

Summary. The Canadian Directive on Automated Decision-Making was revised in
June 2025. The mentioned directive governs the application of automated 
decision-making tools in the process of making or supporting decisions 
pertaining to administrative purposes within federal organizations. These 
measures include algorithmic impact assessment, reporting the results of
the assessments, and introducing appropriate controls depending on the risk level.

Justification and Reflection. I selected Canada because it can be used for 
  comparisons to Australian reforms. The assessment system in Canada demonstrates
how an organization can anticipate risk in advance and update impact assessment
if the system under consideration undergoes changes. I have found out that the
amount of attention needs to correspond to the potential effects on humans. 
In terms of the theory, Act Utilitarianism is a good tool for assessing 
potential danger, whereas Kantianism warns about the loss of fundamental
human rights in cost-benefit analysis.

Overall reflection and comparison
In analyzing these six artifacts, I have realized that ADM is wider
than AI and that government decisions require more than accuracy. In Australia, 
there are plans to implement transparency, review, and human rights protection; 
in the UK, there is a publicly recorded approach to algorithms; in Canada, risk 
assessment is required in relation to the systems that fall under the policy.
All of these methods are different, but all demonstrate that in order to ensure
accountability, there must be procedures in place, not just goodwill.

